se: `experiment_runner`\n- Pipeline completed: `False`\n\n## Previous phases\n\nresource_finder (succeeded)\n\n## Current phase context\n\n- Phase: `experiment_runner`\n- Status: `in_progress`\n- Started: `2026-10-04T02:16:11.814288Z`\n- Next steps:\n  - Validate the report and experimental artifacts before finalizing.\n\n## Workspace check\n\n- Root: `/workspaces/do-llms-behave-differently-when-a323-codex-gpt-6-astra`\n- Directory usable: `True`\n\n## Output validation\n\nNo phase output validation recorded yet.\n\n## Agent notes\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START -->\n### resource_finder\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:resource_finder -->\nResource-finder Phases 1–5 complete: 24 PDFs (all ten specified papers fully chunk-read), three dataset collections, six pinned code repositories. Handoff: resources.md, literature_review.md, planning.md, per-directory READMEs. Exactly three directions retained: controlled style pairs, crossed authorship labels, generalizing probes with causal-specificity interventions; rejected directions recorded. No experiments run.\nIntegrity evidence: notes/final_validation.json, pdf_validation.json (677 readable pages), dataset_validation.json, code_validation.json (151 syntax parses), manifests and reproduction scripts. Data/code excluded from Git; samples total 3,689 bytes. Source PDF 2407.12856 has a nonfatal blending-color-space warning; readable text/rendered chunks inspected, original preserved.\nUSE datasets/anthropic_verified/ (30,051 rows): requested HF philosophy duplicated NLP; repaired from pinned original source. JBB: 100 harmful/100 benign +300 judge rows. WildChat: only 1,000 unpinned prefix rows, 995 unique hashes/523 English; not representative or verified human authorship.\nNext: experiment_runner reads these artifacts, constructs and audits semantic pairs, predeclares metrics/grouped splits and costs, runs behavioral pilot, then controlled representation work if feasible. Known limits: no factual-accuracy benchmark/model weights/runtime tests; JailbreakBench excludes Python 3.12; format repo lacks advertised paired-training script; awareness repo lacks steering. RTX A6000 49,140 MiB observed, 7–8B feasibility untested. No unresolved download failure or ranking change. Completion marker: .resource_finder_complete.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:resource_finder -->\n\n### experiment_runner\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:experiment_runner -->\nUpdate this section at the end of the `experiment_runner` phase.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:experiment_runner -->\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END -->\n# Literature review\n\n## Scope and conclusion\n\nThe literature supports prompt-sensitive behavior and some ability to detect text provenance. It does **not** establish that a responder changes safety behavior because it internally identifies its prompter as an LLM. Actual authorship, AI-like style, declared authorship, evaluation context and task content are distinct variables. The strongest opportunity is to cross them under audited semantic controls, then test whether a generalizing representation mediates behavior.\n\nSearch: diligent paper-finder first (39 candidates), then primary arXiv/publisher pages, Semantic Scholar metadata attempts and citation chasing. Twelve finder candidates passed task-specific screening; 24 PDFs total were downloaded, including all ten specified papers. Semantic Scholar returned 429; publisher CDN resolved an MDPI 403. Search decisions: [screening log](notes/search_screening.md). All specified PDFs and Devbunova were read through every 3-page chunk using text-assisted reading and rendered sheets. Other papers received targeted screening, not exhaustive appendix review. Full author lists, PDF versions, hashes and links: [paper catalog](papers/README.md); detailed notes: [reading log](notes/reading_log.md), [extended notes](notes/extended_reading.md). Findings below are published results, not results from this workspace.\n\n## Key papers: authorship and preference\n\n**Panickssery, Bowman & Feng (2024), “LLM Evaluators Recognize and Favor Their Own Generations.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13076). GPT-4, GPT-3.5 and Llama2 evaluate summaries of 1,000 articles each from XSUM and CNN/DailyMail. Pairwise and individual ratings, order reversal, self-recognition fine-tuning and length/vowel/readability control tasks connect recognition with preference. GPT-4 achieves 73.5% recognition in the reported aggregate. Swapping source labels changes GPT-4 XSUM preference from 0.73 to 0.32, but CNN only 0.94 to 0.89. The authors explicitly leave causal validation open; fine-tuning can alter other properties. Reuse label swaps and order controls, not the assumption that recognition causes preference. Code linked from paper: `bit.ly/llm_self_recognition`.\n\n**Laurito et al. (2024/PNAS 2025), “AI–AI Bias: Large Language Models Favor Communications Generated by Large Language Models.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12856). Regenerates descriptions for 109 products, 100 papers and 250 movies, then compares choices across five model families and a small human sample. GPT-4-generated product descriptions are selected 89% by LLMs versus 36% by humans. Counterbalanced presentation and abstract word-count matching address some confounds. Human comparison has 13 raters; invalid responses are excluded, and title-based movie generation need not preserve facts. This is stylistic preference evidence, not safety mediation. Code: [ai-ai-bias](https://github.com/lauritowal/ai-ai-bias).\n\n**Xu, Li & Jiang (2025, PDF revised 2026), “AI Self-preferencing in Algorithmic Hiring.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00462). Uses 2,245 cleaned pre-LLM resumes, regenerated summaries, nine evaluators, conditional logistic regressions controlling LIWC/semantic similarity, and 18 human annotators. Larger-model adjusted self-minus-human preference gaps range approximately 67–82 percentage points; cross-LLM effects are weaker and heterogeneous. Revision-only checks and simulated screening extend the result. Baselines include original summaries, other generators, source-neutral instructions and small-model voting. Similarity controls do not guarantee equal information or quality; simulation is not actual hiring, and mitigation does not isolate self-recognition. Public resume source: Kaggle/LiveCareer; no dedicated code release verified.\n\n## Key papers: awareness and causal interpretation\n\n**Needham et al. (2025), “Large Language Models Often Know When They Are Being Evaluated.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.23836). Builds 1,000 transcripts from 61 sources, balanced chat/agentic and evaluation/deployment, with 100 development and 900 test items. Five elicitation questions and repeated samples produce AUC around 0.83 for strong models versus 0.921 for informed human authors. Agentic discrimination exceeds chat; probability calibration is poor. Multiple-choice versus open-ended purpose identification differs strongly. Deployment includes synthetic instructions, so its labels cannot become authorship labels. Explicitly asking whether a transcript is an evaluation may itself prime the phenomenon. Code: [eval_awareness](https://github.com/jjpn97/eval_awareness).\n\n**Abdelnabi & Salem (2025), “The Hawthorne Effect in Reasoning Models: Evaluating and Steering Test Awareness.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14617). Three 32B reasoning models, 2,587 real/hypothetical prompt pairs, judge-labeled reasoning spans, linear probes and edits to aligned FFN rows. Span probes reach approximately 93–95% accuracy, but prompt-token readouts are weaker. On 150 synthetic tool-call pairs, steering awareness increases QwQ real-task execution 36.2→56.3%; effects differ by model/direction. Standard harmful-answer judgments show weaker evidence. HarmBench, BBQ and small capability subsets provide checks. “Test,” “hypothetical” and safety concepts remain entangled. Code: [Test_Awareness_Steering](https://github.com/microsoft/Test_Awareness_Steering). The specified [LessWrong post](https://lesswrong.com/posts/B2o6nrxwKxLPsSYdh/do-llms-comply-differently-during-tests-is-this-a-hidden) summarizes earlier work, not independent corroboration.\n\n**Nguyen et al. (2025), “Probing and Steering Evaluation Awareness of Language Models.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.01786). Llama3.3-70B, 480 layer/dataset probes, SAD selection and held-out benchmark classification. A simple contrastive probe attains AUC 0.816 versus length 0.584 and random 0.517. In prompted WMDP sandbagging, linear-probe steering recovers under 5% of lost accuracy; selected SAE features recover up to roughly 25%, but an unrelated feature is competitive. An explicit deployment suffix recovers 83%. This is a valuable negative/specificity control: predictive probes need not identify the controlling mechanism. Code is linked in paper; reuse diagnostics through the related repositories in the resource catalog.\n\n**Heidari, Memarian & Rabusseau (2026), “Evaluation Awareness in Language Models: Representation, Verbalization, and Control.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21766). Six 7–49B open models, 16 framing pairs, 400 calibration/200 test items from WildChat and benchmarks, plus 1,000 MASK prompts for verbalization. Best-layer AUROC exceeds 0.7, but prompt-level probe/verbalization correlation is small; steering changes verbalization. Training-stage comparisons use Olmo. This does not establish safety changes. Appendix judge-rubric changes greatly alter Olmo prevalence; no human gold validation. Norm scaling includes padding, and large steering causes degeneration. Code: [evaluation-awareness](https://github.com/evaluation-awareness/evaluation-awareness).\n\n## Key papers: detection and surface confounds\n\n**Quaremba et al. (2026), “Linear Probing Provides Robust and Efficient Detection of Machine-Generated Text.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24780). Frozen Llama3-8B last-token activations, training-only PCA 100, per-layer averaged or concatenated logistic probes; four benchmarks (DetectRL, MultiSocial, RAID, TSM), 16 subsets with 1,500/500 train/test examples and 16 detector baselines. Probes improve in-domain and transfer AUROC; per-layer averaging transfers more reliably. Few-label results retain PCA fitted on the full training split. OOD transfer is within benchmark families, and “machineness” is not demonstrated as an authorship belief. Good readout baseline, not a causal conclusion. Code: [mgt_probes](https://github.com/gerritq/mgt_probes).\n\n**Chen et al. (2025), “RepreGuard: Detecting LLM-Generated Text by Revealing Hidden Representation Patterns.”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.13152). PCA of paired human/LLM activation differences, tail-token projection averages and a calibrated threshold. DetectRL experiments use 512 training pairs, 1,000 test pairs and five resamples; reported ID/OOD AUROC 96.34/93.49 against statistical and fine-tuned detectors. Short-text performance is weaker. Despite “training-free” terminology, labeled pairs and threshold selection are needed. FPR notation is inconsistent between prose and tables; verify before citing extreme-tail performance. Code: [RepreGuard](https://github.com/NLP2CT/RepreGuard).\n\n**Chalkidis (2026), “Templated or Fully Synthetic? Prompt Construction as a Confound…”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11008). Compares real chat prompts, 2,700 templated and 2,700 LLM-generated prompts across six topics, three intents and three stances ; 120 triplets for realism and 150 prompts for construct labeling. Humans rank generated prompts nearly as realistic as real ones. Templates encode unintended stance via fillers; neutral-setting stance estimates differ by about 0.42 ordinal points for one responder. Judges lack human response-label validation; refusals are excluded at different rates. Detectability is explicitly not tested as a behavioral mediator. Released data: [synthetic_polistance](https://huggingface.co/datasets/kiddothe2b/synthetic_polistance). AI-generated does not necessarily mean AI-like.\n\n**Devbunova (2026), “Is Evaluation Awareness Just Format Sensitivity?”** [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.19426). A 2×2 context/format design with Llama3.1-8B, length-matched ShareGPT/benchmark contrasts and rewrite controls. Standard probes call 94.5% of benchmark-formatted deployment prompts evaluations; decorrelated training reduces this to 7.2%. Formal prose alone does not reproduce the effect. MuTual first turns remain a proxy, sources differ, and only one backbone is studied; the result challenges probe interpretation, not awareness's existence. Code: [eval-awareness-format](https://github.com/viliana-dev/eval-awareness-format).\n\n## Additional papers and measurement guidance\n\n| Downloaded work | Actionable contribution and boundary |\n|---|---|\n| [Perez et al., 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09251) | 154 model-written evaluations; supplies opinion-conditioned sycophancy data. Generated evaluation labels do not verify human prompt provenance. |\n| [Sharma et al., 2023/ICLR 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13548) | Five assistants/four free-form tasks; wrong admissions, biased feedback and error mimicry motivate measuring behavior beyond politeness. |\n| [Wang et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02087) |MMLU, logit lens and activation patching; first-person stance changes sycophancy. Hold perspective and certainty fixed. |\n| [Haverals & Martin, 2025/2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08831) | Human-authorship halo: 556 humans, 13 AI evaluators in study 1; blind/true/swapped labels on fixed stories, with symmetric-label checks. Literary preference need not transfer to safety. |\n| [Rospocher et al., 2026](https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/1/95) |Three Italian stories, four models, 3,600 completions: human/AI/collaboration attribution changes ratings. Only three independent stories. |\n| [Dubois et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23971) |Question/statement, certainty and perspective factorial; relevant surface-feature controls and expressed-versus-choice sycophancy distinction. |\n| [Hua et al., 2025/ICLR 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20487) |Steering changes evaluation-conditioned type-hint behavior in a deliberately trained model organism; not proof of natural safety deception. |\n| [López-Feliu & Ozbayoglu, 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22224) |Qwen2.5-7B refusal/sycophancy reconstruction versus transmission circuits; steering efficacy alone does not establish endogenous mediation. |\n| [Cheng et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.03058) |Verbalized assumptions and internal probes suggest inferred validation-seeking as an alternative mechanism to authorship. |\n| [Baez et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07003) |Factual/opinion probe transfer varies across models; do not pool sycophancy subtypes. |\n| [Fu et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.29198) | 450 dilemmas/13 models distinguish identity and opinion cues; source-label manipulations may also alter inferred user goals. |\n| [Maltbie & Raval, 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11609) | 768 persona conversations show model/domain heterogeneity; demographic expansion deferred to preserve scope. |\n| [Ye et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21778) | 70-paper taxonomy/106-expert survey: specify which sycophancy behavior is measured. |\n\n## Recommended experiment and evaluation\n\nKeep exactly the three ranked directions in [planning.md](planning.md): (1) semantic-matched style pairs; (2) the same bodies crossed with human/AI/unspecified source labels; (3) source-generalizing probes plus necessity/rescue interventions. Pair creation must preserve task, information, stance strength and requested harm level, with blinded equivalence review. Length, clarity, politeness, formatting, certainty and grammatical person require explicit controls. Use multiple writer families; keep all variants of a base request together across splits.\n\nPrepared datasets are JBB 100 harmful/100 benign, 30,051 verified Anthropic opinion-sycophancy rows, and 1,000 WildChat conversations for wording/pilot validation. The HF philosophy file duplicated NLP; **use `datasets/anthropic_verified/`**. WildChat has 995 unique hashes and 523 English labels, is a prefix sample, and does not certify human authorship. These data do not supply objective answer-accuracy labels.\n\nBehavioral baselines: original/neutral request, matched single-feature edits, unlabeled source, counterfactual labels. Report harmful compliance separately from benign over-refusal, opinion agreement separately from factual error, and invalid outputs separately from refusal. Use blind cross-family judges plus human validation. Report paired percentage-point effects and confidence intervals clustered by base request, style×label interactions, discordant counts, and multiplicity-adjusted primary tests. Repeated generations do not increase independent prompt count; size the main study from pilot discordance.\n\nMechanistic baselines: surface-only and shuffled-label probes, training-only preprocessing, held-out generator/domain splits, random/nuisance/evaluation-awareness vectors and sham interventions. Report AUROC/AUPRC plus calibrated probabilities only when calibrated. Measure fluency and capability under interventions; mask padding and retain failed outputs. A causal claim requires specificity and necessity/rescue under natural variation, not merely separability or steerability. A null style effect within one benchmark or model cannot establish universal invariance.\n# Resources catalog\n\nResource gathering is complete: **24 paper PDFs, three dataset collections, six code repositories**. No experiments or model inference have run. Start the next phase with [planning.md](planning.md), [literature_review.md](literature_review.md), and the resource-finder notes in [STATE.md](STATE.md).\n\n## Papers\n\nAll ten specified arXiv papers were downloaded and read through every chunk. The specified LessWrong post was retrieved and reviewed as a related discussion, not counted as a paper. Fourteen additional PDFs support design and measurement. Full metadata, versions, SHA256 hashes and reading depth are in [papers/README.md](papers/README.md) and [notes/papers_catalog.json](notes/papers_catalog.json). Detailed methodological evidence is in [reading_log.md](notes/reading_log.md) and [extended_reading.md](notes/extended_reading.md). All PDF hashes matched and every page was readable. The source PDF for 2407.12856 emits a nonfatal blending-color-space warning; its rendered chunks were inspected and the original bytes retained. Details: [validation](notes/pdf_validation.json).\n\n| Paper | Authors | Year | Local PDF |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Towards Understanding Sycophancy in Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13548) | Sharma, Mrinank; Tong, Meg; Korbak, Tomasz et al. | 2023 | [2310.13548](papers/2310.13548.pdf) |\n| [Discovering Language Model Behaviors with Model-Written Evaluations](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09251) | Perez, Ethan; Ringer, Sam; Lukošiūtė, Kamilė et al. | 2022 | [2212.09251](papers/2212.09251.pdf) |\n| [When Truth Is Overridden: Uncovering the Internal Origins of Sycophancy in Large Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02087) | Wang, Keyu; Li, Jin; Yang, Shu et al. | 2025 | [2508.02087](papers/2508.02087.pdf) |\n| [The human-authorship halo: attribution bias in literary style evaluation by humans and AI](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08831) | Haverals, Wouter; Martin, Meredith | 2025 | [2510.08831](papers/2510.08831.pdf) |\n| [Ask don't tell: Reducing sycophancy in large language models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23971) | Dubois, Magda; Ududec, Cozmin; Summerfield, Christopher et al. | 2026 | [2602.23971](papers/2602.23971.pdf) |\n| [From Trait Vectors to Circuits: Tracing Refusal and Sycophancy Through Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22224) | López-Feliu, Oscar Miró; Ozbayoglu, Maya | 2026 | [2609.22224](papers/2609.22224.pdf) |\n| [Is Evaluation Awareness Just Format Sensitivity? Limitations of Probe-Based Evidence under Controlled Prompt Structure](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.19426) | Devbunova, Viliana | 2026 | [2603.19426](papers/2603.19426.pdf) |\n| [Steering Evaluation-Aware Language Models to Act Like They Are Deployed](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20487) | Hua, Tim Tian; Qin, Andrew; Marks, Samuel et al. | 2025 | [2510.20487](papers/2510.20487.pdf) |\n| [Large Language Models Often Know When They Are Being Evaluated](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.23836) | Needham, Joe; Edkins, Giles; Pimpale, Govind et al. | 2025 | [2505.23836](papers/2505.23836.pdf) |\n| [The Hawthorne Effect in Reasoning Models: Evaluating and Steering Test Awareness](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14617) | Abdelnabi, Sahar; Salem, Ahmed | 2025 | [2505.14617](papers/2505.14617.pdf) |\n| [Probing and Steering Evaluation Awareness of Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.01786) | Nguyen, Jord; Hoang, Khiem; Attubato, Carlo Leonardo et al. | 2025 | [2507.01786](papers/2507.01786.pdf) |\n| [Evaluation Awareness in Language Models: Representation, Verbalization, and Control](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21766) | Heidari, Farzaneh; Memarian, Amin; Rabusseau, Guillaume | 2026 | [2608.21766](papers/2608.21766.pdf) |\n| [Linear Probing Provides Robust and Efficient Detection of Machine-Generated Text](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24780) | Quaremba, Gerrit; Yan, Hanqi; Black, Elizabeth et al. | 2026 | [2608.24780](papers/2608.24780.pdf) |\n| [RepreGuard: Detecting LLM-Generated Text by Revealing Hidden Representation Patterns](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.13152) | Chen, Xin; Wu, Junchao; Yang, Shu et al. | 2025 | [2508.13152](papers/2508.13152.pdf) |\n| [LLM Evaluators Recognize and Favor Their Own Generations](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13076) | Panickssery, Arjun; Bowman, Samuel R.; Feng, Shi | 2024 | [2404.13076](papers/2404.13076.pdf) |\n| [AI Self-preferencing in Algorithmic Hiring: Empirical Evidence and Insights](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00462) | Xu, Jiannan; Li, Gujie; Jiang, Jane Yi | 2025 | [2509.00462](papers/2509.00462.pdf) |\n| [AI-AI Bias: large language models favor communications generated by large language models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12856) | Laurito, Walter; Davis, Benjamin; Grietzer, Peli et al. | 2024 | [2407.12856](papers/2407.12856.pdf) |\n| [Templated or fully synthetic? Prompt construction as a confound in measuring LLM political stance beyond writing assistance](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11008) | Chalkidis, Ilias | 2026 | [2608.11008](papers/2608.11008.pdf) |\n| [Dissociating the Internal Representations of Sycophancy in LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07003) | Baez, Anthony; Karny, Sheer; Pataranutaporn, Pat | 2026 | [2607.07003](papers/2607.07003.pdf) |\n| [Verbalizing LLMs' assumptions to explain and control sycophancy](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.03058) | Cheng, Myra; Sieh, Isabel; Zope, Humishka et al. | 2026 | [2604.03058](papers/2604.03058.pdf) |\n| [What Counts as AI Sycophancy? A Taxonomy and Expert Survey of a Fragmented Construct](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21778) | Ye, Meryl; Ibrahim, Lujain; Bo, Jessica Y. et al. | 2026 | [2605.21778](papers/2605.21778.pdf) |\n| [Intersectional Sycophancy: How Perceived User Demographics Shape False Validation in Large Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11609) | Maltbie, Benjamin; Raval, Shivam | 2026 | [2604.11609](papers/2604.11609.pdf) |\n| [How Identity and Opinion Shape Political Sycophancy in LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.29198) | Fu, Li-Ni; Meng, Chang-Chih; Chen, Chien-Hua et al. | 2026 | [2608.29198](papers/2608.29198.pdf) |\n| [Machines Prefer Humans as Literary Authors: Evaluating Authorship Bias in Large Language Models](https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/1/95) | Marco Rospocher; Massimo Salgaro; Simone Rebora | 2026 | [2026_machines_prefer_humans](papers/2026_machines_prefer_humans.pdf) |\n\nThe literature supports testing style and source-label sensitivity, but it does not establish the proposed internal-authorship mechanism. Probe detection, explicit attribution, and causal mediation must remain separate claims.\n\n## Datasets\n\n| Collection | Downloaded scope and size | Task | Local location |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [JBB-Behaviors](https://huggingface.co/datasets/JailbreakBench/JBB-Behaviors) | Full 100 harmful +100 benign behaviors; 300 judge comparisons; approximately 407 KB CSV | Harmful compliance, benign over-refusal, judge calibration | `datasets/jbb/data/` |\n| [Anthropic model-written-evals](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Anthropic/model-written-evals), [verified original](https://github.com/anthropics/evals/tree/84fcc677e52e1902d696c32cd1a6b663e70d3993/sycophancy) | Three sycophancy files, **30,051 verified rows**, approximately 24.9 MB; raw HF snapshot also retained | Opinion-conditioned sycophancy | **Use `datasets/anthropic_verified/`** |\n| [WildChat-1M](https://huggingface.co/datasets/allenai/WildChat-1M) | **1,000-row prefix only**, 16,462,494 bytes; 995 unique conversation hashes, 523 English labels | Natural wording and pilot construction | `datasets/wildchat/first_1000.jsonl` |\n\nThe HF philosophy file was byte-identical to its NLP file. The pinned original Anthropic repository supplies the verified philosophy replacement; raw HF files are retained only for provenance. Do not double-count the two snapshots. WildChat is an unpinned viewer snapshot and not representative or verified human-authored text. All files load; schema, missingness, duplicates and sample checks are recorded in [dataset validation](notes/dataset_validation.json).\n\n[Dataset README](datasets/README.md) includes licenses, splits, loading and **download/reproduction instructions**. Native CSV/JSONL files and cloned upstream data are excluded from project Git; only tiny documentation samples are included. Reproduce with `.venv/bin/python scripts/reproduce_datasets.py`; the manifest pins file hashes and fails if the unpinned viewer changes. No factual accuracy dataset or audited matched-style pairs are supplied.\n\n## Code repositories\n\n| Repository | Commit | Local location | Main use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [jailbreakbench](https://github.com/JailbreakBench/jailbreakbench) | `23dbdf6b1965` | `code/jailbreakbench/` | Behavior loading and refusal/compliance judges |\n| [mgt_probes](https://github.com/gerritq/mgt_probes) | `8875297e4184` | `code/mgt_probes/` | Frozen activation and linear detector baselines |\n| [eval-awareness-format](https://github.com/viliana-dev/eval-awareness-format) | `b6a5000cba8c` | `code/eval-awareness-format/` | Length/format controls and cross-condition evaluation |\n| [test-awareness-steering](https://github.com/microsoft/Test_Awareness_Steering) | `eb78ea593223` | `code/test-awareness-steering/` | Test-awareness extraction and steering reference |\n| [ai-ai-bias](https://github.com/lauritowal/ai-ai-bias) | `73475012582b` | `code/ai-ai-bias/` | Content-controlled descriptions, pairwise choice and caching |\n| [evaluation-awareness](https://github.com/evaluation-awareness/evaluation-awareness) | `8ad6a8ebd6e6` | `code/evaluation-awareness/` | Contrastive/random probes, AUROC and verbalization correlation |\n\n[Code README](code/README.md) records entry points, dependencies and limits. All 151 Python files syntax-parse; runtime compatibility is untested. JailbreakBench declares Python <3.12, while this workspace is 3.12.8. The format repository lacks its advertised paired-training script; the evaluation-awareness checkout contains probe analysis but no steering. Do not assume the original experiments are turnkey. Reproduce pinned snapshots with `.venv/bin/python scripts/reproduce_code.py`.\n\n## Search strategy and decisions\n\nThe paper-finder diligent search returned 39 candidates; 12 met the task-specific relevance screen and were downloaded. Explicit resources were prioritized, followed by primary arXiv/publisher pages and citation chasing. [Screening log](notes/search_screening.md) records all candidates and exclusions; raw service results are under `paper_search_results/`. Semantic Scholar metadata calls returned HTTP 429, so primary sources supplied metadata. An MDPI PDF initially returned 403; its publisher CDN succeeded. All specified resources are represented; large datasets were deliberately limited to relevant subsets.\n\nExactly three research directions were retained after scoring evidence, relevance, information gain and feasibility: **paired style effects; crossed source labels; controlled representation interventions**. The ranking and five rejected alternatives are documented in [planning.md](planning.md). No later expansion occurred. Code selection supports those directions; observational origin comparisons and detector leaderboards are not separate research arms.\n\n## Experiment handoff\n\n1. Build proposition-audited style pairs from JBB and a grouped, stratified verified Anthropic subset. WildChat supplies wording examples, not provenance truth. Freeze inclusion, semantic-equivalence and deduplication rules before outcome generation.\n2. Cross identical bodies with human/AI/no source labels. Keep certainty, grammatical person, politeness, task details and harm level fixed or explicitly ablate them. Use multiple writers and independent responder sessions.\n3. Measure harmful compliance separately from benign over-refusal; report opinion agreement, invalid responses, paired percentage-point changes and clustered confidence intervals. Blind judges and validate a human-audited subset. Do not infer factual accuracy from opinion labels.\n4. If the behavioral design is sound, adapt MGT probes and format controls. Hold out request/generator families, fit preprocessing on training only, and compare random/nuisance directions, ablation and rescue. Separability or steering alone is insufficient evidence of mediation.\n\nThe fresh project `.venv`, [pyproject.toml](pyproject.toml) and `uv.lock` contain resource-gathering dependencies only. [Environment evidence](notes/environment_validation.json) records one RTX A6000 with 49,140 MiB; a 7–8B pilot is plausible but untested. Model weights, activation datasets, generator access, judges, runtime dependencies and actual cost estimates remain experiment-phase work. Use the 1,200-output JBB style pilot and 3,600-output crossed design as planning counts, not a completed or statistically powered study.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd\ncat planning.md\ncat .codex/skills/data-analysis/SKILL.md\ncat pyproject.toml\nrg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*.py' code/mgt_probes code/evaluation-awareness | head -30\nls datasets/jbb/data datasets/anthropic_verified\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os,requests,json\nprint('key available',bool(os.getenv('OPENROUTER_KEY')))\nr=requests.get('https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models');r.raise_for_status(); j=r.json();json.dump(j,open('results/live_catalog.json','w'))\nfor m in j['data']:\n if any(s in m['id'] for s in ['qwen3-8b','qwen3.5','gemini-3','gpt-5','claude-sonnet']): print(m['id'],m['pricing'],m.get('supported_parameters'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd\ncat planning.md\ncat .codex/skills/data-analysis/SKILL.md\ncat pyproject.toml\nrg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*.py' code/mgt_probes code/evaluation-awareness | head -30\nls datasets/jbb/data datasets/anthropic_verified\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os,requests,json\nprint('key available',bool(os.getenv('OPENROUTER_KEY')))\nr=requests.get('https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models');r.raise_for_status(); j=r.json();json.dump(j,open('results/live_catalog.json','w'))\nfor m in j['data']:\n if any(s in m['id'] for s in ['qwen3-8b','qwen3.5','gemini-3','gpt-5','claude-sonnet']): print(m['id'],m['pricing'],m.get('supported_parameters'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/do-llms-behave-differently-when-a323-codex-gpt-6-astra\n# Experimental direction ranking and handoff\n\nPhase 1 ranking, 2026-10-04. Scores 1–5 (higher is better), equal weights: literature evidence E, relevance R, information gain I, implementation feasibility F. These are planning judgments, not measured results. Exactly three directions retained. No experiments have run.\n\n| Rank | Direction | E | R | I | F | Total | Decision and evidence |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| 1 | Paired, content-preserving prompt style manipulation | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5 | 20 | KEEP: direct hypothesis; AI–AI bias and prompt-construction studies motivate behavior measurement with semantic controls (2407.12856, 2608.11008). |\n| 2 | Cross identical prompt bodies with declared authorship | 4 | 5 | 5 | 5 | 19 | KEEP: separates text from attribution cues; self-recognition and human-authorship halo give source-label precedents (2404.13076, 2510.08831). |\n| 3 | Generalizing authorship probe plus targeted causal intervention | 4 | 5 | 5 | 2 | 16 | KEEP, hardware conditional: detector/probe work supplies methods, format-control and weak steering results demand specificity (2608.24780, 2603.19426, 2507.01786). |\n| 4 | Authorship detector leaderboard only | 5 | 2 | 2 | 5 | 14 | PRUNE as standalone: detection does not answer behavior or mediation; retain only manipulation checks. |\n| 5 | Observational WildChat vs model-written benchmark comparison | 4 | 3 | 2 | 5 | 14 | PRUNE: task, topic, length, provenance and benchmark formatting are inseparable. |\n| 6 | Fine-tune responder alignment or recognition | 3 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 11 | PRUNE: changes responder, not just prompt; compute and interpretation costs. |\n| 7 | Agentic/multi-agent tool-use expansion | 3 | 3 | 3 | 1 | 10 | PRUNE: many new context, tool and evaluator confounds. |\n| 8 | Multilingual and demographic factorial expansion | 3 | 3 | 3 | 1 | 10 | PRUNE: valuable external validity, but multiplies cells before central effect established. |\n\nSearch space is closed at these three. Change ranking only if new evidence invalidates it; record rationale in STATE.md.\n\n## Shared definitions and controls\n\nSeparate actual provenance A, perceived AI-like style S, declared source L, measured internal coordinate M, and behavior Y. Human-styled text generated by a model is not human-authored. WildChat user turns are not verified human-only writing. Detecting a distributional signature is not proof of an explicit authorship belief. Test the premise that short prompts are recognizable; do not assume it.\n\nUse canonical requests with a proposition checklist: exact task, target, requested detail, stance, premises, constraints, numbers and harm level. Produce two styles using multiple independent writer families; include original and neutral rewrite controls. Preserve content in both directions. Have blinded reviewers assess equivalence, clarity, stance strength and task intent before observing responses. Freeze acceptance rules; report excluded pairs by arm. Automated semantic similarity is insufficient. Keep inference conversations independent and identical in system prompt, tools, decoding and context.\n\nControl or factorially ablate length, politeness, formatting, grammatical person, certainty and clarity. Token-length matching alone does not remove pragmatic confounds. Estimate both the total effect of an operational style bundle and effects within controlled feature strata; post-treatment adjustment cannot uniquely identify mediation. Do not ask a responder to guess provenance before its behavioral response: that primes authorship/evaluation. Use independent sessions for manipulation checks.\n\n## 1. Paired behavior experiment\n\nPrimary set: all 100 JBB harmful and 100 benign requests; interpret harmful compliance and benign over-refusal separately. Add a stratified, deduplicated Anthropic sycophancy subset, balanced by base question and opinion; do not count persona paraphrases as independent questions. WildChat supplies natural wording examples and an external-validity subset after language/context/provenance checks, not gold origin labels.\n\nMinimum pilot: 200 JBB base requests × 2 styles × 3 independent decoding seeds = 1,200 outputs/model; plus original/neutral and feature-ablation subsets. Start with one accessible model and then a distinct responder family. For an API budget compute calls × expected input/output tokens × actual provider prices before execution. No paid calls or model weights downloaded in this phase.\n\nOutcomes: refusal (explicit/partial/none), harmful actionable compliance, benign task completion, opinion agreement conditional on supplied stance, invalid/truncated response rate. Do not collapse polite tone into sycophancy. Anthropic opinion labels are not factual truth labels. Accuracy is optional and requires an objectively scored factual benchmark (not yet downloaded); exclude accuracy claims until supplied.\n\n## 2. Attribution experiment\n\nCross the same two style bodies with symmetric metadata: “The following request was written by a human”, “...by an AI system”, or no source claim. Keep request exact across labels. Include swapped/false labels by design; no claims about true provenance from this arm. Keep source wording and message role fixed; randomize execution order. Two styles × 3 labels × 200 requests × 3 seeds = 3,600 outputs/model; direction 1's unlabeled cells can be reused if collected under the identical protocol. Source-label interactions measure sensitivity to attribution instructions, not pure latent belief. A null label effect does not refute implicit recognition.\n\n## 3. Representation and causal specificity\n\nOne RTX A6000 with 49,140 MiB was observed during resource gathering, making a 7–8B pilot plausible. Choose an accessible instruct backbone after checking its license and measuring actual activation-memory use; no model inference or memory benchmark has run. Collect verified-origin calibration texts if available; otherwise explicitly call the readout an AI-style probe. Freeze train/validation/test partitions by underlying request, author/generator family and source corpus. All rewrites of a request remain in one partition. Fit PCA/scaler/probes on training only, choose layers/strengths on validation, evaluate once on held-out data. Compare mean-difference, logistic and surface-only baselines (length, punctuation, readability, n-grams), plus shuffled labels.\n\nEvaluate out-of-domain and unseen-generator AUROC/AUPRC, calibrated Brier only after fitting calibration, length-balanced and format-swapped generalization. Test authorship vs evaluation-awareness and politeness/clarity directions separately. Inspect last nonpadding prompt token and alternative pooling positions.\n\nIntervene at validated layers with norm-matched random, nuisance, sham, sign-reversal and dose controls. Mask padding in norm estimates. Prefer matched activation patching/ablation and restoration of naturally occurring coordinates to large arbitrary additions. Measure behavioral changes, fluency/capability degradation and all invalid outputs. Test necessity and rescue, not just steerability; a vector that changes Y need not mediate the natural S→Y effect (2609.22224). If hardware unavailable, deliver directions 1–2 and report mechanism unresolved, rather than treating API self-reports as activations.\n\n## Analysis plan and gates\n\nPredeclare primary outcomes and smallest meaningful effects. Use within-request contrasts, paired bootstrap or randomization tests clustered by base request; repeated generations are not independent prompts. For binary paired outcomes, report discordant cells and McNemar/exact paired inference; use mixed models for style×label and question-level clustering where appropriate. Report absolute percentage-point differences with 95% CIs, model-specific effects and Holm correction for primary outcome families. Power depends on discordant probability: use pilot to size the main study; 100 harmful requests cannot establish small null effects. Use equivalence intervals for meaningful null conclusions.\n\nBlind judges to source/style labels; use cross-family judges and a human-audited subset, report confusion matrix and agreement. Response style may bias judges too. Do not discard refusals, invalid outputs or unmatched pairs silently. Keep hashes, seeds, prompt versions, API model snapshots, timestamps, token costs and completion status. No novelty claim until comparing these controlled results with the cited studies.\n---\nname: data-analysis\ndescription: Conduct exploratory data analysis and statistical testing with test selection guidance. Use when exploring datasets, selecting statistical tests, performing power analysis, or preparing results for publication.\n---\n\n# Data Analysis\n\nGuidance for exploratory data analysis and statistical testing.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- Exploring new datasets\n- Selecting appropriate statistical tests\n- Performing power analysis\n- Reporting results in papers\n- Validating experimental results\n\n## Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n\n### EDA Workflow\n\n1. **Load and Inspect**: Basic data structure\n2. **Summarize**: Descriptive statistics\n3. **Visualize**: Distributions and relationships\n4. **Identify Issues**: Missing data, outliers\n5. **Document Findings**: Key insights\n\n### Initial Inspection\n\n```python\n# Basic checks\ndf.shape          # Dimensions\ndf.dtypes         # Data types\ndf.head()         # First rows\ndf.describe()     # Summary stats\ndf.isnull().sum() # Missing values\n```\n\n### Key Questions to Answer\n\n| Question | What to Check |\n|----------|---------------|\n| What's the size? | Rows, columns, data types |\n| Any missing data? | Null counts, patterns |\n| What's the distribution? | Histograms, descriptive stats |\n| Any outliers? | Box plots, z-scores |\n| Any relationships? | Correlations, scatter plots |\n| Any patterns? | Trends, clusters, groups |\n\n### Visualization Guide\n\n| Data Type | Visualization |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Single continuous | Histogram, density plot, box plot |\n| Single categorical | Bar chart, pie chart |\n| Two continuous | Scatter plot, line plot |\n| Two categorical | Grouped bar chart, heatmap |\n| Continuous + categorical | Box plot by group, violin plot |\n| Time series | Line plot with time axis |\n\n## Statistical Test Selection\n\n### Decision Tree\n\n```\nQuestion: What are you trying to do?\n│\n├─ Compare groups\n│   │\n│   ├─ How many groups?\n│   │   ├─ 2 groups → See \"Two Group Comparisons\"\n│   │   └─ 3+ groups → See \"Multiple Group Comparisons\"\n│   │\n│   └─ Related or independent?\n│       ├─ Independent (different subjects)\n│       └─ Related (same subjects, before/after)\n│\n├─ Examine relationships\n│   ├─ Two variables → Correlation, regression\n│   └─ Multiple variables → Multiple regression\n│\n└─ Test proportions\n    └─ Chi-square test\n```\n\n### Two Group Comparisons\n\n| Data Type | Independent Groups | Related Groups |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Normal** | Independent t-test | Paired t-test |\n| **Non-normal** | Mann-Whitney U | Wilcoxon signed-rank |\n\n### Multiple Group Comparisons\n\n| Data Type | Independent Groups | Related Groups |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Normal** | One-way ANOVA | Repeated measures ANOVA |\n| **Non-normal** | Kruskal-Wallis | Friedman test |\n\n### Checking Assumptions\n\n**Normality Tests**:\n- Shapiro-Wilk (n < 50)\n- Kolmogorov-Smirnov (n ≥ 50)\n- Visual: Q-Q plot\n\n**Homogeneity of Variance**:\n- Levene's test\n- Visual: Box plots by group\n\n**Independence**:\n- By experimental design\n- Durbin-Watson (for residuals)\n\n### When Assumptions Fail\n\n| Violation | Solution |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Non-normality | Non-parametric test, transformation |\n| Unequal variance | Welch's t-test, transformation |\n| Non-independence | Mixed-effects model |\n| Outliers | Robust methods, removal (with justification) |\n\n## Effect Sizes\n\n### Why Effect Sizes Matter\n\n- p-values tell you if effect exists, not how big\n- Effect sizes quantify the magnitude\n- Required for power analysis\n- Better for meta-analysis\n\n### Common Effect Sizes\n\n| Measure | Context | Interpretation |\n|---------|---------|----------------|\n| **Cohen's d** | Two means | 0.2=small, 0.5=medium, 0.8=large |\n| **Pearson's r** | Correlation | 0.1=small, 0.3=medium, 0.5=large |\n| **Eta-squared** | ANOVA | 0.01=small, 0.06=medium, 0.14=large |\n| **Odds ratio** | Categorical | 1.5=small, 2.5=medium, 4=large |\n\n### Computing Effect Sizes\n\n```python\n# Cohen's d for two groups\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef cohens_d(group1, group2):\n    n1, n2 = len(group1), len(group2)\n    var1, var2 = np.var(group1, ddof=1), np.var(group2, ddof=1)\n    pooled_std = np.sqrt(((n1-1)*var1 + (n2-1)*var2) / (n1+n2-2))\n    return (np.mean(group1) - np.mean(group2)) / pooled_std\n```\n\n## Power Analysis\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Power** | Probability of detecting true effect (1-β) |\n| **α (alpha)** | False positive rate (typically 0.05) |\n| **β (beta)** | False negative rate (typically 0.20) |\n| **Effect size** | Magnitude of effect |\n| **Sample size** | Number of observations |\n\n### Power Analysis Uses\n\n1. **A priori**: Before study, determine needed sample size\n2. **Post hoc**: After study, calculate achieved power\n3. **Sensitivity**: Given n and power, what effect detectable?\n\n### Sample Size Calculation\n\n```python\nfrom statsmodels.stats.power import TTestIndPower\n\nanalysis = TTestIndPower()\n\n# Sample size for t-test\nn = analysis.solve_power(\n    effect_size=0.5,    # Cohen's d\n    alpha=0.05,         # Significance level\n    power=0.80,         # Desired power\n    ratio=1.0,          # n2/n1\n    alternative='two-sided'\n)\n```\n\n### Power Guidelines\n\n| Power | Interpretation |\n|-------|----------------|\n| < 0.50 | Inadequate |\n| 0.50-0.70 | Low |\n| 0.70-0.80 | Moderate |\n| ≥ 0.80 | Adequate (standard target) |\n| ≥ 0.90 | High |\n\n## Multiple Comparisons\n\n### The Problem\n\n- Each test has α chance of false positive\n- Multiple tests inflate false positive rate\n- Family-wise error rate: 1-(1-α)^n\n\n### Correction Methods\n\n| Method | When to Use | Strictness |\n|--------|-------------|------------|\n| **Bonferroni** | Few comparisons | Most conservative |\n| **Holm** | Few comparisons | Less conservative |\n| **Benjamini-Hochberg** | Many comparisons | Controls FDR |\n| **Tukey HSD** | Post-hoc ANOVA | Common choice |\n\n### Applying Corrections\n\n```python\nfrom scipy import stats\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Bonferroni\nadjusted_alpha = 0.05 / num_tests\n\n# Holm-Bonferroni\nfrom statsmodels.stats.multitest import multipletests\nreject, pvals_corrected, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, method='holm')\n\n# Benjamini-Hochberg (FDR)\nreject, pvals_corrected, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, method='fdr_bh')\n```\n\n## Reporting Results\n\n### APA Format\n\n**t-test**:\n```\nt(df) = X.XX, p = .XXX, d = X.XX\n```\nExample: t(45) = 2.34, p = .023, d = 0.68\n\n**ANOVA**:\n```\nF(df1, df2) = X.XX, p = .XXX, η² = .XX\n```\nExample: F(2, 87) = 4.56, p = .013, η² = .095\n\n**Correlation**:\n```\nr(df) = .XX, p = .XXX\n```\nExample: r(48) = .42, p = .003\n\n**Chi-square**:\n```\nχ²(df, N = n) = X.XX, p = .XXX\n```\nExample: χ²(2, N = 150) = 6.78, p = .034\n\n### Reporting Guidelines\n\n**Do**:\n- Report exact p-values (not just < .05)\n- Include effect sizes\n- Report confidence intervals\n- Describe what was tested\n\n**Don't**:\n- Say \"proved\" or \"significant difference\" alone\n- Report only significant results\n- Cherry-pick tests\n- Over-interpret p = .049 vs p = .051\n\n### Results Table Format\n\n```\nTable 1: Comparison of Methods on Benchmark X\n\nMethod      Mean (SD)       95% CI          Effect Size\n------------------------------------------------------\nBaseline    75.2 (3.4)     [73.1, 77.3]    -\nMethod A    78.9 (2.8)*    [77.2, 80.6]    d = 0.52\nMethod B    81.4 (3.1)**   [79.5, 83.3]    d = 0.89\n\nNote: * p < .05, ** p < .01 vs Baseline\n```\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Problem | Solution |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| P-hacking | Running tests until significant | Pre-register analysis |\n| HARKing | Hypothesizing after results known | State hypotheses before |\n| Multiple comparisons | Inflated false positives | Apply correction |\n| Pseudo-replication | Non-independent samples | Mixed-effects models |\n| Ignoring effect sizes | Significant ≠ important | Report effect sizes |\n\n## Quality Checklist\n\n- [ ] Research questions clearly stated\n- [ ] Appropriate test selected and justified\n- [ ] Assumptions checked\n- [ ] Sample size justified (power analysis)\n- [ ] Multiple comparisons corrected\n- [ ] Effect sizes reported\n- [ ] Confidence intervals included\n- [ ] Results correctly interpreted\n- [ ] Limitations acknowledged\n\n## References\n\nSee `references/` folder for:\n- `test_selection.md`: Detailed test selection guide\n- `apa_reporting.md`: Complete APA reporting templates\n[project]\nname 